.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_DYNAMIC_ALLOCS_COUNT 3 "2026-09-15" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count \- report outstanding fallback allocations
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the number of outstanding allocations that came from the
system allocator instead of the pool's own storage. They happen only in
a pool created with fallback_to_dynamic_memory = true, and only after
the pool's elements run out.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of outstanding fallback allocations, which is 0 for
a pool created without a fallback
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A non-zero result means the pool was too small for its workload at
some point, and that elem_count should go up: every fallback
allocation is a round trip to the system allocator, which is exactly
the cost the pool is there to avoid.

An entry in a thread's cache belongs to the pool, so this call never
counts one.

ccol_mempool_destroy() treats an outstanding fallback allocation as a
leak and stops the process, so free every fallback entry of a pool
before you destroy it.

A NULL mp is a fatal error: the call prints a message and ends the
process with abort(), in every build.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the pool's lock, so it belongs on a cold path; don't
use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
